window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"08-02","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"08-02-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-01/","title_he":"א – מעלת האמת","title_en":"1. The Virtue of Truth","body_en":"<p>One of the most fundamental traits, on which the world stands, is the trait of truth (Avot 1:18). Much guidance is brought in the Torah regarding the obligation to tell the truth and to keep far from falsehood. In the following halakhot we will deal with this weighty subject, bringing practical examples of how much one must make an effort in order to tell the truth, and in which case it is permitted according to halakha to deviate slightly from the truth. We will open first by explaining the virtue of truth.</p>\n<p>Our ability to progress in this world and in the World to Come depends on understanding reality. The intellect is the most important tool at a person's disposal, and the better we understand reality, the better we will be able to progress. But the central problem is that reality is deep and complex, and there are deep layers in it that our limited human intellect has difficulty grasping, and therefore all of us suffer to one degree or another from a lack in recognising the truth. There is a gap between what we know about each thing and the things as they truly are. And since our recognition of reality is not complete, there sometimes arises for us a contradiction between what seems in our eyes good and beneficial and what is moral and just. The better we recognise true reality, the more the imagined gap between what is moral and what is beneficial and pleasant will narrow, and through this we will be more truthful and just, and it will be better for us in our lives. Striving for truth is striving for identity between understanding, speech and reality.</p>\n<p>The one who knows the truth fully is of course the Creator of the world, and therefore the Sages said (Shabbat 55a): \"The seal of the Holy One, blessed be He, is truth.\" The seal is the most unique expression of the one who seals; and since the Holy One, blessed be He, knows reality absolutely, therefore His seal in the world is 'truth'.</p>\n<p>And the more precious truth is to us, and we are even ready to sacrifice benefits for its sake, the more we will draw close to the trait of the Holy One, blessed be He, and thus we will merit to understand reality better; and out of drawing close to the Holy One, blessed be He, and to the understanding of the world, we will merit to progress and rise. And this is the mitzva of the Torah: \"And you shall walk in His ways\" (Devarim 28:9).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-02/","title_he":"ב – איסורי השקר השונים","title_en":"2. The Various Prohibitions of Falsehood","body_en":"<p>The general prohibition of lying includes within it different shades of lies, all of which of course are forbidden by the Torah, for we are commanded to cleave to the traits of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said (Devarim 28:9): \"And you shall walk in His ways\", and one of the traits of the Holy One, blessed be He, is truth. And nevertheless the Torah related in greater detail to several areas.</p>\n<p>One prohibition is mentioned in the verse \"Keep far from a false matter\" (Shemot 23:7), and its subject is not to describe an event that happened in the past with altered details. And likewise regarding the future, a person is forbidden to describe his plans with altered details. And the more harm the lie causes his fellow, the more severe it is; and all the more so if it is said during testimony in a court, its severity is very great. But also lies that cause no harm to others, such as mere small lies in describing an event, are included in this prohibition.</p>\n<p>The second prohibition is the prohibition of 'geneivat da'at' (deception), that is, not to deceive one's fellow by an act or speech that causes the fellow to think as though we did something for his sake, when in practice we did nothing. The difference between this prohibition and the prohibition of lying is that the prohibition of lying is directed towards describing something in the past or the future, which must be said precisely, whereas the prohibition of 'geneivat da'at' is directed towards conduct in the present, which must be straight, without performances intended to create a false impression. The source of the prohibition is in the verse \"You shall not steal\" (Vayikra 19:11); and although generally in our language theft is the taking of an object or money, nevertheless we find in Scripture several times that deception too is called theft, as it is said of Avshalom that he stole the heart of Israel (II Shmuel 15:6; we will devote the next chapter to the prohibition of 'geneivat da'at').</p>\n<p>The third prohibition is flattery. There are two main kinds of flattery: the first, that although a certain person deserves condemnation for an act he did, his fellow flatters him to his face and denies the truth. And the second, that a person behaves towards his fellow as though he loves and admires him, but his heart is far from love and appreciation for him, and all his flattery is only to win his support and help. The source of the prohibition of flattery is in the verse: \"So you shall not pollute (taḥanifu) the land\" (Bamidbar 35:33).</p>\n<p>The fourth prohibition belongs to matters of business: all speech in matters of business must be said with honesty. Therefore a person is forbidden to tell his fellow that in the future he will buy from him or sell him something, when in his heart he does not intend to do so. And even if at the time he said it he intended to fulfil his words, only afterwards circumstances changed and it is hard for him to fulfil them, he must make an effort to keep his word. The source for this is in the verse (Vayikra 19:36): \"Just balances, just weights, a just efa and a just hin (hin tzedek) shall you have\", and the Sages explained that the verse intends to teach us that our 'yes' (hen) should be yes and our 'no' should be no; that a person is forbidden to say one thing with his mouth and another in his heart, and likewise that he should fulfil his words and keep his word (Bava Metzia 49a; Encyclopedia Talmudit, 'Hen Tzedek').</p>\n<p>There is a special fifth prohibition concerning witnesses, that they not lie in their testimony, and this is one of the Ten Commandments, as it is said (Shemot 20:13): \"You shall not bear false witness against your fellow.\" There is enormous importance in the court being able to arrive at the truth, in order to establish justice firmly and to found the life of society on the values of justice and uprightness; and therefore the Torah established a special prohibition for witnesses not to lie, and even included it in the Ten Commandments.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-03/","title_he":"ג – שני סוגי שקר","title_en":"3. Two Kinds of Lies","body_en":"<p>There are two main kinds of lies. There are lies that harm others: for example, a person who borrowed money and denies it, or a person who goes to testify false testimony against his fellow in order to help another friend. And likewise one who is consulted about whether it is worthwhile to buy an apartment or a car or any other product, and he does not tell the truth because he has a personal interest that that person buy or not buy the product – in all these lies the liar causes harm to his fellow. And sometimes the harm is not measured in money but in causing distress to the fellow; for example, a person who reports to his fellow a false report about a certain event in order to stir up strife between two friends. Or even one who does not want his fellow to enjoy a certain trip or outing, and for this he lies and paints for him a distorted picture of that trip or outing. And even a woman who gives her friend a wrong recipe so that she will not succeed as she does in baking the cake or the dish – this too is a lie in which there is harm and causing distress to another. The more harm the lie causes another, the more severe it is.</p>\n<p>The second kind of lies are those that do not cause monetary harm or distress to another; rather, the liar's aim is to draw heightened attention to his stories, and therefore he alters his stories or adds invented details to them. And although he does not thereby cause real harm to his friends, nevertheless there is a prohibition in this. The main harm caused by these lies is in creating a counterfeit picture of the world. Such a person will in the end reach the point where he cannot understand anything in reality thoroughly, because through his lies he becomes accustomed not to attribute much importance to facts.[1]</p>\n<p>Both kinds of lies are forbidden, but the fundamental difference between them is that where the lie will cause harm to another, in no case is there a permission to lie. But regarding the second kind of lie, which causes no harm to another, there are cases in which the Sages permitted deviating from the truth, for example in order to prevent an insult or in order to make peace between a person and his fellow (Sha'arei Teshuva, gate 3, 181).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"According to the author of Sefer Ḥaredim (positive commandments of the mouth, 26), the verse \"Keep far from a false matter\" applies also to these lies; and according to the author of Sefer Yere'im, mitzva 235, this verse applies only to lies that cause harm, since the continuation of the verse is \"and the innocent and righteous do not slay\" (Shemot 23:7). But one must know that even if the prohibition of telling a lie that causes no monetary harm is not learned from the verse \"Keep far from a false matter\", it is clear that its basis is from the Torah. For it is a Torah mitzva: \"And you shall walk in His ways\" (Devarim 28:9), that is, to cleave to His traits, and one of the main traits through which the Holy One, blessed be He, is revealed in the world is the trait of truth, as the Sages said (Shabbat 55a): \"The seal of the Holy One, blessed be He, is truth.\" If so, the whole dispute is only over the particular question of whether the Torah devoted a special verse to forbid all lies, or whether there is general guidance about this without a special verse.<br>However, it is possible that the practical significance of this dispute will be in those cases in which it is permitted to deviate, for the sake of modesty and the like. For if there is a verse from the Torah, only in rare cases will it be possible to be lenient and deviate; and if there is no explicit verse, it will be easier to be lenient in borderline cases in which there is a clash between the value of truth and the value of modesty and of preventing shame and the like."}]},{"slug":"08-02-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-04/","title_he":"ד – גנות השקר","title_en":"4. The Disgrace of Falsehood","body_en":"<p>In tractate Sota (42a) it is explained that there are four kinds of people who do not merit to see the face of the Shekhina, and one kind among them is 'the liars' – those who are accustomed to lie (the others: flatterers, scoffers, and tellers of lashon ha-ra).</p>\n<p>And the point is that whoever becomes accustomed to lying harms first and foremost himself. And even if generally his lies do not harm others, but he likes to lie in order to draw attention, or because he prefers to alter his stories in order to glorify his name and hide his weaknesses, and so he tells what he would have liked to happen but not exactly what happened – such a person creates for himself a counterfeit picture of the world, in which his personal imagination carries more weight than the facts, and out of this he distances himself from the ability to understand the world as it is. The matter resembles a person who needs drugs in order to enjoy his life; so the liar needs to alter reality in order to be able to live in it. In practice, of course, reality will not change, and only he, the liar, gradually loses his connection to life.</p>\n<p>And as a result of his becoming accustomed to relating to reality in an unreliable way, the most severe thing of all happens to him: he will not be able to reach true faith. Only a person who becomes accustomed to relating to reality itself will be able to perceive the most absolute and true reality in the world – the One who gives life to and sustains all of reality, the Creator, blessed be He.</p>\n<p>And this is what the Sages said, that liars do not merit to see the face of the Shekhina. There are many levels in seeing the Shekhina, and in every small spark of faith there is a certain aspect of seeing the Shekhina, in whose merit a person will merit a more revealed seeing of the Shekhina in the world of truth. But one who lies cannot merit this, because God, blessed be He, is revealed through absolute reality. To the degree that a person cleaves to the trait of truth, and is ready even to sacrifice incidental gains for its sake, to that same degree he will be able to merit the trait of faith in this world and the revelation of the Shekhina in the World to Come.</p>\n<p>Incidentally, in this world too, in the long term one who cleaves to the trait of truth will succeed more. For despite the difficulty involved, he will learn to cope with reality as it is, and will succeed more in realising his strengths. But one who learns to lie always looks at the world in a distorted way, and so will never learn to cope with the challenges before him.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-05/","title_he":"ה – דוגמאות לדברי שקר","title_en":"5. Examples of False Speech","body_en":"<p>In the Talmud, tractate Shevu'ot (31a), interesting examples of the prohibition of falsehood are brought, and the rule is that not only a complete lie is forbidden, but anything in which there is a trace of falsehood is forbidden, as it is said (Shemot 23:7): \"Keep far from a false matter.\"</p>\n<p>For example, what should a faithful worker do whose employer turns to him with a request to lie for him? Or, to sharpen the question: what should a student do whose revered teacher turned to him and said: \"You know, my student, that I speak the truth, and even if they gave me all the wealth in the world I would not let a lie escape my mouth. But you know that I gave so-and-so money as a loan, and now he denies it and claims that he took nothing from me. Unfortunately, I have only one witness who saw the loan, and as is known, only on the basis of two witnesses can he be obligated to pay me. Therefore please, since you know that I speak the truth, come to the court and testify too that he owes me money.\"</p>\n<p>According to halakha, of course the student is forbidden to testify in favour of his teacher or his employer, for how can he say, \"I saw that so-and-so borrowed money\", when he did not see it?! And although he is certain that his teacher is right, he is forbidden to testify about something he did not see, as it is said (Shemot 20:13): \"You shall not bear false witness against your fellow.\"</p>\n<p>And not only this, but even if his teacher told him: do not let a lie escape your mouth; only come to the court and give the impression as though you intend to testify in my favour that my debtor owes me money – and then perhaps the lying debtor will be deterred and admit the truth, because he will say in his heart, \"Better for me to admit it myself and not be obligated by the verdict\" – even this is forbidden. For after all, the student gives the impression as though he is preparing to lie, and the Torah said: \"Keep far from a false matter\", that is, not only should you not let a lie escape your mouth, but you should even keep far from appearing as one who is about to lie.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-06/","title_he":"ו – שפת אמת תכון לעד","title_en":"6. The Lip of Truth Shall Be Established for Ever","body_en":"<p>Sometimes a person may face a hard dilemma: on the one hand, the obligation to tell the truth; and on the other, if he tells the truth he will not achieve what seems to him just.</p>\n<p>For example, it is related in the Talmud (Shevu'ot 31a) about three people who lent money to a certain person, each of them giving him a hundred gold coins. Afterwards the borrower stood before them and lied brazenly, claiming that he had borrowed nothing from them. The problem is that each of the three gave him the loan without witnesses and without a document, so that in practice, if they come to court to claim their debt, the court will not be able to do anything, for there is no proof on the basis of which one should prefer the version of the plaintiffs over the version of the defendant.</p>\n<p>The question is whether they are permitted to lie in order to receive what is due to them. For example, they could agree among themselves that one of them would claim three hundred gold coins from this borrower, and the other two would come before the court as witnesses and say that they saw how so-and-so lent such-and-such three hundred gold coins. And after the plaintiff wins the case, he would give a hundred gold coins to each of the witnesses, and thus each of them would receive what is due to him, and the lying defendant would pay his debt and not come out on top.</p>\n<p>According to halakha they are forbidden to do so, because it is forbidden to lie, and all the more so it is forbidden to testify false testimony before the court. And although in principle they are right, they are forbidden to obtain their money by means of a lie, and it is better that they lose their money and not disrupt the rules of justice. Next time it would be better for them to be less naive, and to lend their money before witnesses or with a document. Indeed, they should sue him as far as they are able, so that the sinner does not profit; and perhaps the court in its wisdom will succeed in bringing him to admit it, and if not, at least it will become known that there is doubt about his reliability.</p>\n<p>Here it is important to say that one who speaks the truth, even though sometimes in the short term telling the truth will cause him losses, will in the end come out ahead. And this is what is said (Mishlei 12:19): \"The lip of truth shall be established for ever, but a lying tongue is but for a moment.\" And the reason that \"the lip of truth shall be established for ever\" is that the one who speaks the truth is faithful to absolute reality, and out of this his existence is firm, and all the winds in the world will not be able to move him from his place and nullify him. But one who alters the truth a little has already become detached from reality as it is, and his existence is shaky, and all his gains will not endure in the long term.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-07/","title_he":"ז – מדבר שקר תרחק בבית-הדין","title_en":"7. Keep Far from a False Matter in Court","body_en":"<p>The most severe lies are the lies told in court. The whole purpose of judgement is to fight falsehood, to eradicate injustices and to arrive at the truth, in order to vindicate the righteous and to find the guilty liable. And every lie told in court harms the verdict, and harms our ability to resolve the disputes between a person and his fellow according to justice and uprightness.</p>\n<p>Therefore, in addition to the general prohibition of lying, there is a special warning to witnesses not to bear false testimony, as it is said (Shemot 20:13, in the Ten Commandments): \"You shall not bear false witness against your fellow.\" And therefore before the testimony they would warn the witnesses not to lie, and admonish them before the public, and inform them of the severe punishment of false testimony in this world and in the World to Come.</p>\n<p>And there is a further command: whoever saw something that may be of use to a proceeding being held in court, whether his testimony will cause his fellow's acquittal or his liability, the witness is obligated to come and testify about what he saw, in order to help the court clarify the truth. And if he does not come to testify, he nullifies a positive commandment, and about him it is said (Vayikra 5:1): \"If he does not utter it, then he shall bear his iniquity.\" And there is a difference in this law between monetary cases and capital cases. In capital cases, even if they did not ask him, he is obligated to come and testify; and in monetary cases he is obligated to come by force of this mitzva only if they asked him to come and testify (Sefer Ha-Ḥinukh 122). However, by force of the mitzva \"Neither shall you stand idly by the blood of your fellow\" (Vayikra 19:16), also in monetary cases one who knows testimony through which he can save his fellow's money is commanded to come and testify in his favour (Rambam's Sefer Ha-Mitzvot 297; Sefer Ha-Ḥinukh 237).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-08/","title_he":"ח – חובת הדיינים להגיע לאמת","title_en":"8. The Duty of Judges to Arrive at the Truth","body_en":"<p>Although a person must always beware of letting a lie escape his mouth, a double and redoubled caution from any trace of falsehood must be exercised by witnesses and judges, and several Torah mitzvot were said about this.</p>\n<p>The Sages said in tractate Shevu'ot (30b) that if the judges see that according to all the rules of law it emerges that so-and-so is liable, but their heart tells them that there is some kind of fraud here – for example, that the witnesses are lying, or that the plaintiff did not tell the witnesses the whole truth, and they in their innocence understood one thing while the truth was another – in such a case it is incumbent on the judges not to make do with keeping the protocol and taking testimony in the usual way as in all monetary cases. Rather, they must question the witnesses again and again, and all the details of the case, until they uncover the lie. And if they did not succeed in exposing any fraud, and nevertheless their inner feeling is that the verdict they must issue according to the rules of law does not accord with the truth, in such a case they must withdraw from the case and say that it is not in their power to rule on this question, because they feel that the ruling they would have to issue is not correct. And the source for this is in the verse (Shemot 23:7): \"Keep far from a false matter\", that is, not only is an actual lie forbidden, but the mitzva is to keep far from falsehood, so that the judges may be certain that no mistaken judgement will issue from their hands (Rambam, Sanhedrin 24:3).</p>\n<p>The Sages further said that a student who sits before his teacher at the time of judgement and sees that his teacher is erring in his reasoning is forbidden to keep silent out of respect for his teacher; rather, he must rise and say his opinion, so that the teacher can weigh his words, and through this the judgement will be clarified properly. By contrast, the judges are forbidden to allow an ignorant student to sit before them at the time of judgement, because that student is liable to say foolish arguments and thereby divert the judges from the search for truth, and the Torah said, \"Keep far from a false matter\" (Shevu'ot 31a).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-09/","title_he":"ט – המקיים דבריו ומחוסר אמנה","title_en":"9. One Who Keeps His Word, and One Who Lacks Faithfulness","body_en":"<p>Of all the actions a person does, speech is what most expresses our uniqueness as human beings. Without speech human society could not exist and develop, and the intellect would remain in its raw potential without the ability to come to actuality. And therefore Onkelos translates the verse \"And man became a living soul\" as \"a speaking spirit\" (Bereshit 2:7), that is, the vitality in a person is expressed in his ability to speak.</p>\n<p>In order to preserve our human dignity, we are obligated to relate to speech with holy awe and to fulfil all that comes out of our mouth. And so we learned in the mishna (Shevi'it 10:9): \"Whoever keeps his word – the spirit of the Sages is pleased with him.\" And the reason is that he relates with due seriousness to his words.</p>\n<p>For example, a person who told his fellow that he would give him a gift must make an effort to keep his word. And if he did not give it, he is according to the definition of the Sages among those who lack faithfulness, that is, an unreliable person. However, if he said that he would give his fellow a large gift and in the end did not give it, he is not considered one who lacks faithfulness, because it was clear from the outset that, since one is speaking of a large gift, he would give it only if he could. But if he said that he would give an ordinary gift, it is clear that he indeed intends to give it, and if he did not give it, he is among those who lack faithfulness (Bava Metzia 49a; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav, Mekhira 2).</p>\n<p>And if already at the time he told his fellow that he would give him a gift he intended not to keep his word, this is more severe, because already at the time of speaking he lied. And the Sages said (Pesaḥim 113b) that there are three kinds of people who sin in matters of speech whom the Holy One, blessed be He, hates, and the first of them is one who speaks one thing with his mouth and another in his heart.</p>\n<p>The same law applies to every arrangement between a person and his fellow. For example, if he told his fellow that he would help him in the evening, he is commanded to keep his word and come to help. And if already at the time he said it he intended in the end to evade and not help, this is more severe, because not only did he not keep his word, but he also intended from the outset to lie.</p>\n<p>Of course, a craftsman too who committed himself to finish his work by a certain date must according to the law keep his word.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-10/","title_he":"י – הן צדק במסחר","title_en":"10. An Honest 'Yes' in Commerce","body_en":"<p>Commerce is the basis for a life of material comfort. A person alone cannot properly provide for all his needs; and only thanks to commerce can each person concentrate on one occupation in which he can become professionally proficient, and afterwards, in return for his work, buy all his needs from others. In short, commerce is the basis for a proper life. Therefore, in addition to the mitzva always to tell the truth, the Torah added a special command concerning telling the truth in business dealings.</p>\n<p>And so it is said (Vayikra 19:36): \"Just balances, just weights, a just efa and a just hin shall you have; I am the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt.\" And the Sages expounded that the Torah intends to teach us in the command \"a just hin\" that, in addition to the measuring instruments needing to be precise, speech too must be precise and true: that 'yes' be yes and 'no' be no (Bava Metzia 49a).</p>\n<p>Therefore a seller in a shop is obligated to tell the customer the truth. For example, if the customer asks for the refrigerator by the end of the month, and the seller knows that there is no chance that the refrigerator will arrive before that date, he must tell the customer so. And if he lied to the customer and said something that in his heart he knows he will not be able to fulfil, he transgresses the Torah's command \"a just hin\". And indeed the test is hard, for it is possible that if he tells the customer that the refrigerator will not arrive by the end of the month, the customer will prefer to buy in another shop. But telling the truth is more precious, and in the end a seller who keeps his word will gain a good name, and it is reasonable that in the long term his profits will grow.</p>\n<p>And if at the time the seller undertook to supply the refrigerator by the end of the month he did intend to keep his word, but in the course of time he was negligent and did not keep his word – although he did not intend to lie, nevertheless in this too the seller transgressed a prohibition, and he is called in the language of the Sages 'one who lacks faithfulness', that is, an unreliable person, and the spirit of the Sages is not pleased with him, as it is said (Tzefanya 3:13): \"The remnant of Israel shall not do iniquity, nor speak lies.\" And certainly through his negligence he transgressed the general command \"And you shall walk in His ways\" (Devarim 28:9), that is, to walk in the ways of God and to cleave to His good traits, one of the most central of which is the trait of truth and reliability.</p>\n<p>However, regarding the mitzva \"a just hin\", which was said regarding business dealings, there are views among the poskim. According to several of the great Rishonim the obligation \"a just hin\" establishes that it is forbidden to say one thing with the mouth and another in the heart; but if the seller intended to keep his word, even though afterwards he was negligent and did not keep it, he did not transgress the command \"a just hin\" (Rosh; Ramban in the Milḥamot on Bava Metzia 49a). And some say that even if he did not keep his word because of negligence, he transgresses the mitzva \"a just hin\" (Ha-Ma'or there).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-11/","title_he":"יא – מחוסר אמנה במכירת מכונית","title_en":"11. Lacking Faithfulness in Selling a Car","body_en":"<p>Question: A person agreed with a buyer who saw his car that he would sell it to him in the evening at a certain price. Afterwards another buyer came who is willing to pay more. Is the seller obligated to sell the car to the first one at the agreed price, or is he permitted to sell the car at a higher price to the second buyer?</p>\n<p>Answer: According to halakha the seller must sell his car to the first buyer at the price that was agreed, because their agreement, although it was made in words alone, obligates him morally. And indeed, if he wants to break his word and sell the car at a higher price, to the first or to the second, it will not be possible to sue him in court and compel him to keep his word, because no binding act of acquisition (kinyan) has yet been made, nor has a document been signed. But the Sages defined him as one who lacks faithfulness, that is, a person who cannot be believed. Only if it was said explicitly that the agreement is not binding is the seller permitted to raise the price of the car.</p>\n<p>And if between the agreement in words and the time of the sale there was a rise in car prices, or the exchange rate changed, so that the seller can claim, \"Had I known that the prices or the exchange rate would change, I would not have agreed to this\" – in such a case there are two views. Some say that although the data have changed, they are forbidden to retract from the agreement in words between them. And some say that since the data have changed, they are permitted to retract from the agreement. (See Sma 204:12; the first view is that of the Rambam, SA and Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 204:8, and the second that of the Rosh and the Rema; and see Dinei Mamonot by Rav Batzri, vol. 2, p. 1.)</p>\n<p>And likewise, for example, a person who inquired in a shop about buying an oven and told the seller that he would come in the evening to buy it: if this is the only oven of that kind left, the seller is forbidden to sell it to someone else, and likewise the buyer is forbidden to go and buy the oven in another shop or to retract from his words. Only if the agreement was in non-binding language, for example he said, \"It looks like I'll come this evening to pay for the oven and take it\", is the seller permitted to sell it to another buyer, and the buyer is likewise permitted not to come in the evening.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-12/","title_he":"יב – מידת חסידות בדבקות באמת","title_en":"12. The Trait of Piety in Cleaving to Truth","body_en":"<p>In order to understand the special caution of the great ones of Israel from any trace of false speech, and even from false thought, one must first explain the virtue of truth.</p>\n<p>When a person describes events precisely and keeps his word, he becomes more connected to reality as it truly is, and through this he is able to recognise the One who created it. But when a person lies and says what is convenient for him to say, he becomes detached from reality, and everything he builds will collapse, because he is not connected to reality itself. Even Jerusalem, our holy city, was destroyed because people of faithfulness, that is, reliable people, ceased from it. And although they believed in the Torah and studied it, since they lied in their business dealings and did not tell the truth, they became detached from reality, and Jerusalem was destroyed (Shabbat 119b).</p>\n<p>And so Rabbi Elazar said: \"Whoever changes his word is as if he worships idolatry\" (Sanhedrin 92a). Just as one who worships idolatry denies the Creator of the world, so the liar denies reality.</p>\n<p>And since the trait of truth is so precious, even though according to the law only an undertaking in speech is binding, nevertheless it is a custom of piety to fulfil even thoughts. Therefore a person who wants to sanctify himself and walk in the ways of piety should be careful to fulfil his decisions. It is important to explain that the intention is not that he must fulfil every thought that comes into his mind; rather, the intention is that if he made up his mind and decided finally that he will do such and such, in matters of mitzva or in matters between a person and his fellow, it is a trait of piety that he fulfil his thought.</p>\n<p>And so it is related of Rav Safra that he wanted to sell one of his possessions, and a certain man came before him while he was reciting the Shema and said to him: sell me the object for fifty gold coins. But since Rav Safra was reciting the Shema, he could not answer him. The buyer thought that apparently the sum he had offered was not acceptable to Rav Safra, and therefore he offered again a higher sum.</p>\n<p>After he finished the Shema, he said to the buyer: give me fifty gold coins as you offered at first, and take the object. For already when you offered the first sum I agreed in my heart to sell it to you for this sum. And since Rav Safra spoke truth in his heart, he did not want to break his first thought (Makkot 24a, and Rashi).</p>\n<p>Rav Safra lost many gold coins, but through his cleaving to the trait of truth he merited to cleave to the traits of the Holy One, blessed be He, and through this he merited to reach a higher capacity of faith and a better recognition of reality.</p>\n<p>But as said, this is a trait of piety; only an undertaking in speech obligates a person to keep his word. And even an undertaking in speech creates a personal moral obligation, but the court still cannot compel a person to keep his words. Only if he made an act of acquisition or signed a contract can the court obligate him to fulfil his undertaking.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-13/","title_he":"יג – מתי מותר לסטות מאמירת האמת","title_en":"13. When It Is Permitted to Deviate from Telling the Truth","body_en":"<p>After we have explained at length the value of truth and the prohibition of falsehood, the central question is what we must do in a case where there is a clash between the value of truth and another value. For example, what should one do in a case where someone asks us whether so-and-so said bad things about him? From the side of truth we would need to say: \"Yes, so-and-so spoke against you.\" But from the side of peace and the prohibition of lashon ha-ra, one must deny the fact that so-and-so spoke against him.</p>\n<p>In two places in the Talmud it is explained that it is permitted to deviate from telling the truth for the sake of fulfilling other values. In tractate Yevamot (65b) it is explained that it is a mitzva to deviate for the sake of peace. And in tractate Bava Metzia (23b) it is explained that it is permitted to deviate from telling the truth in three matters: in a tractate (masekhta), in bed (puria) and in hospitality (ushpiza). \"In a tractate\" means in matters of study; \"in bed\" means in intimate marital matters; \"in hospitality\" means in describing one's host. Further on (in halakhot 16-17) we will explain the three matters mentioned here. But first we will deal with the rules of the halakha – which situations it is permitted to lie in – and we will explain three fundamental rules:</p>\n<p>The first: everything the Sages said that it is permitted to deviate applies specifically when the lie does not cause harm to any person. But if through the lie we perhaps spare one person unpleasantness but cause shame or harm to another, it is certainly forbidden to deviate (Sha'arei Teshuva, gate 3, 181).</p>\n<p>The second rule was explained by one of the great Rishonim, Rabbi Yitzḥak Alfasi, namely that there are cases in which it is a mitzva to deviate and there are cases in which it is permitted to deviate. And the difference is between the two sources in the Talmud. In tractate Yevamot one is speaking of a lie for the sake of making peace, and in order to make peace not only is it permitted to lie but it is even a mitzva. But in tractate Bava Metzia one is speaking of deviating from the truth for the sake of preserving modesty or in order to prevent shame, and in these matters it is permitted to deviate but there is no mitzva (Rif on Bava Metzia 23b).[2]</p>\n<p>A third rule is brought in the name of the Ba'al Shem Tov, and logic too requires accepting it, namely: a person who has stumbled in telling lies is forbidden to deviate from the truth even for the sake of peace, because he needs to go to the furthest extreme in telling the truth; for if not, it is clear that he will rule leniently for himself and continue to lie also in forbidden matters (Porat Yosef, parashat Vayetze). Together with this it is clear that there is no permission in this to speak lashon ha-ra or to cause disputes; rather, he should not tell a lie in order to make peace.</p>\n<p>A further important rule should be noted: even when it is permitted to deviate from telling the truth so as not to harm other values, one must reduce the falsehood as much as possible. And therefore the Sages called this 'to deviate' (leshanot), and their intention is that only what is necessary to change so as not to harm other values is permitted, and more than that is forbidden.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"See Perisha ḤM 262:21, who holds that also for the sake of humility or modesty it is a mitzva to deviate, but in order to prevent shame to oneself they permitted deviating, though there is no mitzva."}]},{"slug":"08-02-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-14/","title_he":"יד – מצווה לשנות מפני השלום","title_en":"14. It Is a Mitzva to Deviate for the Sake of Peace","body_en":"<p>Although there is a great obligation to speak the truth, nevertheless for the sake of peace it is permitted and even a mitzva to deviate from the truth.</p>\n<p>So the Sages learned in tractate Yevamot (65b) from the words of Yosef's brothers: after the passing of our father Ya'akov, the brothers feared that perhaps Yosef would want to take revenge on them for having sold him to the Ishmaelites; and therefore, although Ya'akov had told them nothing, for the sake of peace they deviated from the truth and said to Yosef: \"And they sent a message to Yosef, saying: Your father commanded before he died, saying: So shall you say to Yosef: Forgive, I pray you now, the transgression of your brothers and their sin, for they did evil to you; and now, we pray you, forgive the transgression of the servants of the God of your father…\" (Bereshit 50:16-17). And from here it is learned that it is permitted to deviate for the sake of peace.</p>\n<p>And even the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself deviated for the sake of peace. For when our mother Sara was told that a son would be born to her, she laughed within herself and said: how, after I have become old, shall I give birth, and my lord is old. That is, Sara did not believe what her ears heard because both she and Avraham were already old. But when the Holy One, blessed be He, told Avraham that Sara did not believe the tidings of the son, He mentioned only that she said that she herself was old. And this is because every person is hurt when he hears that his wife thinks he is old, and therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, changed her words. And from here we learn that it is a mitzva to deviate for the sake of peace.</p>\n<p>The reason it is permitted to lie for the sake of peace is that the root truth is that all human beings are united at their foundation: one Creator created them, and one purpose is for all of them, and only the complications of the world caused disputes and quarrels. If so, when there is a clash between the value of peace and the value of truth, in fact there is a clash between inner truth and outer truth. For from the side of outer truth there is indeed hostility and hatred between them, but from the side of inner truth they are united in their souls and want to love one another. And when there is such a clash, the Torah instructed to prefer inner truth over outer truth. And therefore the halakha was ruled that it is permitted to lie for the sake of peace. And likewise Aharon the Kohen, who would make peace between a person and his fellow and between a man and his wife (as brought in the next halakha), would deny the bad deeds they had done to each other, for peace is the greater truth.</p>\n<p>Ideally, of course, it is better not to use the permission to lie. Therefore if, for example, a person came and asked his fellow, \"Did so-and-so speak bad things about me?\", it is better to steer the conversation in other directions. But if he insists and demands to know whether they spoke bad things about him, it is permitted to say a complete lie for the sake of peace (Ḥafetz Ḥayim, Laws of Rekhilut 1:8).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-15/","title_he":"טו – רק למען שלום אמת מותר לשנות","title_en":"15. Only for the Sake of True Peace Is It Permitted to Deviate","body_en":"<p>It is a mitzva to make peace between a person and his fellow, between a man and his wife, between family and family, and between state and state (Torat Kohanim, Kedoshim, parasha 11). And unlike many mitzvot, which a person must fulfil only if they happen to come to his hand, the mitzva of peace must be pursued actively, as it is said: \"Seek peace and pursue it\" (Tehillim 34:15; Derekh Eretz Zuta, chapter on peace).</p>\n<p>And because of the greatness of the mitzva to make peace, the halakha was ruled that it is permitted to lie for the sake of peace.</p>\n<p>And so it is related in Avot de-Rabbi Natan (12:3) about Aharon the Kohen, who was a lover of peace and a pursuer of peace: when he heard about two people who had quarrelled with each other, Aharon went and sat with one of them. He said to him: \"My son, see what your friend is saying – he is beating his heart and tearing his clothes, and saying: Woe is me, how shall I lift my eyes and see my friend? I am ashamed before him, for I am the one who wronged him.\" And so he would sit with him until he removed the jealousy from his heart. And Aharon would go and sit with the other and say to him: \"My son, see what your friend is saying – he is beating his heart and tearing his clothes, and saying: Woe is me, how shall I lift my eyes and see my friend? I am ashamed before him, for I am the one who wronged him.\" And so he would sit with him until he removed the jealousy from his heart. Afterwards, when they met each other, they embraced, hugged and kissed each other. And so he would make peace between a person and his fellows and between a man and his wife, and therefore when he passed away it is said (Bamidbar 20:29): \"And they wept for Aharon thirty days, all the house of Israel.\"</p>\n<p>But it is important to know that all we have said – that it is permitted to deviate for the sake of peace – applies specifically when no harm is caused to anyone. But if for the sake of peace someone's rights are denied, it is forbidden to lie. For example, if a person damaged his fellow, one must tell the one damaged about it so that he can sue him in court. And likewise if it is known of someone that he is about to cheat his fellow, one must warn the innocent one against the cheat. In such cases one must not say that for the sake of peace it is better to keep silent; on the contrary, it is a mitzva to tell the truth, in order to save the oppressed from the hand of his oppressor, as it is said (Vayikra 19:16): \"Neither shall you stand idly by the blood of your fellow.\"[3]</p>\n<p>And likewise Aharon the Kohen, had he known that his words would influence only one of the parties to the dispute, would not have deviated for the sake of peace, for only harm and not peace would come of it. For the good person would believe in his innocence that his fellow no longer plots evil against him and would trust him; and in the end the bad person, waiting for the first opportunity to take revenge on his fellow, would exploit the trust placed in him to repay him evil for good. Therefore only when it is clear that through the lie there will truly be peace is it permitted to deviate for the sake of peace. But when it is not clear that this will be so, of course it is forbidden to lie.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"And therefore also one who pleads in defence of politicians who lie for the sake of 'peace' is not correct. For according to many this is not true peace. And moreover, in these agreements there is harm to the rights of the people of Israel to its Land and a violation of the mitzva of settling the Land. And therefore, even if in their opinion this is true peace, they are forbidden to impose their view on those of the other opinion by means of lies."}]},{"slug":"08-02-16","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-16/","title_he":"טז – שלושת הדברים שמותר לשנות בהם","title_en":"16. The Three Matters in Which It Is Permitted to Deviate","body_en":"<p>Now we will clarify the three matters that the Sages listed in tractate Bava Metzia (23b) in which it is permitted to deviate from the truth.</p>\n<p>The first is \"in a tractate\", and Rashi explained that for the sake of humility it is permitted to deviate in matters of a tractate: if a person is asked whether he has learned a certain tractate well with all the commentators, even though he knows it thoroughly, he can answer that he does not know it well, because the trait of humility is that a person should not himself tell of his virtues and the extent of his knowledge. And it is fitting to add that the Aḥaronim wrote that just as it is permitted to act with the trait of humility and to conceal one's knowledge in a tractate, so one who fasts or does other acts of piety is permitted to deviate from the truth in order to conceal his deeds from people (Magen Avraham OḤ 565:7).</p>\n<p>The second matter in which they permitted deviating is \"in bed\", that is, in matters of the modesty of the marital bed, for one should not speak of these intimate matters. Therefore if he is asked about them, he is permitted to deviate and conceal the truth (and similarly, it is permitted to conceal a pregnancy at its beginning).</p>\n<p>And the third is \"in hospitality\", that is, in matters of hosting. If a guest is asked, \"With whom did you stay, and did they receive you pleasantly?\" – if the guest has a concern in his heart that following his words many guests will come to the householder, and this will burden his host, the guest is permitted to deviate in his words and blur the identity of his host. And even if the questioners have learned who hosted him, if he thinks that his hosts prefer that he not tell their praise, so that undeserving people will not descend upon them and consume their money, the guest is permitted to deviate from the truth and say that they treated him coldly. And although we learned (Berakhot 58a) that it is fitting for a good guest to praise the householder, nevertheless this is specifically before decent people; but before people who are liable to exploit the host's good heart for bad, one should not praise. And about this the Sages said (Arakhin 16a): \"He that blesses his friend with a loud voice, rising early in the morning, it shall be counted a curse to him\" (Tosafot there).</p>\n<p>In the past, when food was not found in abundance, there was indeed a danger that a story in praise of the host could cause him harm. But today the concern that such a story will cause harm is small, and there is almost no need to use the permission to deviate in matters of hospitality. However, there are similar cases in which one needs to use this permission. For example, a person who is always ready to help others, and even when he has no time and the help comes at the expense of his family and his livelihood, he will agree to help – one should not tell his praise before undeserving people, lest they exploit his good heart without consideration for his family.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-17","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-17/","title_he":"יז – למניעת בושה","title_en":"17. To Prevent Shame","body_en":"<p>At times a person is permitted to deviate from the truth in order to prevent shame to himself. We learned that it is permitted to deviate \"in a tractate\", and Rashi explained that a person is permitted to conceal his knowledge out of humility. But according to the Rambam the intention is that if a person is asked, \"Are you now studying tractate Shabbat?\", for example, and he is afraid that they will ask him halakhic questions that he will not know how to answer, he is permitted to say that he is studying another tractate, in order to avoid the shame of not knowing the answer (Laws of Robbery 14:13).</p>\n<p>And on this basis one can learn that if a person is travelling to a hospital for a test that he is embarrassed about, such as a fertility test, and someone asks him why he is travelling, he is permitted to conceal the precise purpose of his journey.</p>\n<p>And in any case the author of Sefer Ḥasidim (642) wrote that it is always better not to say an actual lie, but to use language that can be understood two ways.</p>\n<p>But it is very important to repeat and emphasise that the whole permission to deviate from the truth is on condition that the lie will not cause harm, shame or distress to someone else. Therefore two partners, for example, who are speaking about their business affairs – it is clear that each must tell the other only the truth. And even if he is embarrassed to tell about his mistakes, he is obligated to tell the whole truth, because the partner needs to know everything connected to the business and to the quality of his partner's work; and if he lies he will harm his fellow and transgress a Torah prohibition. Only if it is clear to him that if he tells about his mistake, which is not so critical, it will cause a breach of the peace between them, and because of this their business too is liable to be harmed, and his partner will lose precisely from the very telling of the truth – in such a case the partner can rule leniently for himself and deviate for the sake of peace.</p>\n<p>And likewise it is clear that a pupil at school or in high school is forbidden to lie to his teacher. The whole permission to deviate from the truth in order to prevent shame concerns people between whom there is no binding relationship; but the teacher must know the extent of the pupil's progress, so that with joint forces they can work diligently at his progress in study. Therefore if he asks the pupil whether he revised for the exam, the pupil must tell only the truth. Only if the pupil is embarrassed before the whole class is it possibly permitted for him to blur the embarrassing truth in public, but afterwards he should come before his teacher and tell him the truth.</p>\n<p>And likewise a soldier in the army, in everything connected to reliability and operational functioning, is obligated to tell the truth, and there is no permission at all to deviate because of shame; for if he does not tell the truth he will harm the whole system of reliability in the army and damage its operational capability (Ha-Tzava Ke-Hilkhata, ch. 29).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-18","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-18/","title_he":"יח – כלה נאה וחסודה","title_en":"18. A Beautiful and Graceful Bride","body_en":"<p>Question: What should a person do whose friend bought a piece of furniture and asks him for his opinion? Should he say what he really thinks – that the furniture is badly made and will apparently soon fall apart, and its price is high, and in addition its appearance is ugly? Or is he permitted to answer with words of politeness, such as that the furniture is quite good and quite successful, and in general it does not look bad?</p>\n<p>Answer: In such a case there is no mitzva at all to tell the truth, because if he tells the truth he will cause his friend distress, and he has already bought the furniture, and what is crooked cannot be made straight. Therefore ideally it is better not to speak about the furniture at all. But if nevertheless the friend asks and expects an answer, for the sake of politeness and derekh eretz it is fitting to praise it to a certain degree, in order to make him happy. And this is included in what the Sages said, that it is a mitzva to deviate for the sake of peace. And if it is possible to find some particular virtue in the furniture, it is better to concentrate on it.</p>\n<p>And likewise regarding a wedding, Beit Hillel said that when one dances before the bride, one should praise her and say that she is \"a beautiful and graceful bride\". And indeed Beit Shammai disagreed with them and said: what if the bride is lame or blind – must one then too praise her as \"beautiful and graceful\"? Rather, according to Beit Shammai it is forbidden to praise her for something that is not in her; and if the bride is ugly, it is better to keep silent and not say something untrue, or to find some praiseworthy thing in her which they will mention, and not merely say words of falsehood. But even according to Beit Shammai, if one of the family members comes and asks, \"How in your opinion is the bride?\", one certainly must praise her. Only if they were not asked, it is better according to Beit Shammai not to say untrue things needlessly (Tosafot Ketubot 17a, s.v. 'kalla').</p>\n<p>But according to Beit Hillel, in any case one should praise the bride and say that she is \"beautiful and graceful\", for if they do not praise her on her wedding day, everyone will understand that this is because she is not worthy of praise. And since there is a mitzva to increase peace and love among people, it is a mitzva to praise the bride and say that she is \"beautiful and graceful\". And even though in the eyes of most people she is not beautiful, it is possible that in the eyes of her husband she is beautiful and graceful. And in practice the halakha is like Beit Hillel, and a custom has even been accepted in Israel to sing at weddings the words of Beit Hillel brought in the baraita in tractate Ketubot (17a): \"How does one dance before the bride? A beautiful and graceful bride.\"</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"08-02-19","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/08-02-19/","title_he":"יט – העתקה בבחינה","title_en":"19. Copying in an Exam","body_en":"<p>Question: Since many pupils copy in exams, is it permitted to copy in exams? And likewise, is one who has already copied obligated to tell the teacher? And what is the law for one who copied in a matriculation (bagrut) exam?</p>\n<p>Answer: It is certainly forbidden to copy in exams, and there are several aspects to the prohibition. First, there is deception here, for the teacher or examiner thinks that this is the knowledge of the pupil, and in truth the pupil does not know these answers by himself. And according to many Rishonim the prohibition of deception is from the Torah, as it is said (Vayikra 19:11): \"You shall not steal\", and deception is included in this (Ritva, Ḥullin 94a).</p>\n<p>And even for those who hold that this verse is not a source for the prohibition of deception, nevertheless it is certainly forbidden to copy, for we were commanded to walk in the ways of the Creator, that is, to cleave to His traits, as it is said (Devarim 28:9): \"And you shall walk in His ways\", and one of the central traits is 'truth', and the very seal of the Holy One, blessed be He, is 'truth' (Shabbat 55a).</p>\n<p>And although in the short term it may appear as if one who guards himself from the prohibition of copying in exams loses out, and as a result his grade will be lower than those at his level who transgressed and copied, nevertheless truth is more precious than the grade. And it is clear that in the long term one who is careful not to copy in exams will succeed more in his life, because in this way he will learn the most important foundation for his personal advancement, namely to rely only on his own knowledge and efforts, and through this, in the end, his level will be higher. Not so one who is accustomed to copy: he does not learn to invest and make an effort, and at a certain stage in his life it will probably become clear to him that the main person deceived by him was himself.</p>\n<p>And even if by copying in the matriculation exams he succeeds in paving his way to one of the prestigious faculties at the university, it will not help to make him more capable and knowledgeable. And what benefit will grow for him if in the end he will be a mediocre doctor, who will have to hide his lack of knowledge from his patients all his days? And what kind of life will he have as a poor engineer, always toiling to hide his lack of talent from his clients and colleagues? For every person it is better to know which profession does not suit him, for then he will be able to choose a profession in which he can excel.</p>\n<p>And one who copied in an exam, and as a result received a higher grade and will be able to be accepted to places to which he would not have been accepted without this grade, should consult with his rav how to act. For at times through this deception he has taken the place of his fellow who did not copy and received a lower grade than him, and one must consider how the injustice he caused can be corrected. And in any event it is right to instruct one who copied that he study the exam material again and become worthy of receiving the grade he was given as a result of his copying.</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
